Docker is an open-source platform that lets you package an application and all of its dependencies into a container. Containers make it easy to run software consistently across different environments (your laptop, a server, or the cloud).

Why use Docker?
Consistency: "It works on my machine" problems are reduced.
Portability: Run the same container on Windows, macOS, Linux, or cloud platforms.
Lightweight: Containers share the host OS kernel, making them faster and smaller than virtual machines.
Isolation: Applications run independently without conflicting with each other.
Key concepts
Image: A blueprint for a container (like a template).
Container: A running instance of an image.
Dockerfile: A text file containing instructions to build an image.
Docker Hub: An online repository for sharing Docker images.

  Docker vs Virtual Machine
Docker (Containers)	Virtual Machine
Shares host OS kernel	Includes a full guest OS
Starts in seconds	Takes minutes to boot
Lightweight	Heavier, uses more resources
Best for microservices and app deployment	Best for complete OS isolation


  Containers share the host OS kernel,

means

It means Docker containers do not have their own complete operating system.

For example, suppose your computer/server is running Linux:

Your Linux Server
│
├── Docker
│   ├── Container 1 → Java application
│   ├── Container 2 → MySQL
│   └── Container 3 → Nginx
│
└── Linux Kernel  ← shared by all containers
What is the kernel?

The kernel is the core part of the operating system. It manages things like:

CPU
Memory
Files
Network
Processes

A Docker container uses the host machine's kernel instead of bringing its own kernel.

VM is different

A virtual machine contains a complete OS:

Physical Server
│
├── VM 1 → Guest Linux OS → Application
├── VM 2 → Guest Linux OS → Application
└── VM 3 → Guest Linux OS → Application

So:

Container → shares host kernel → lightweight

VM → has its own OS/kernel → heavier


