docker build -t myapp .    The . means current directory is the build context.
Build context means:

The files and folders that Docker is allowed to access during docker build.

For example:

myapp/
├── Dockerfile
├── app.py
└── requirements.txt

If you run:

docker build -t myapp .

The . means:

"Use this myapp directory as the build context."

So Docker can access:

app.py
requirements.txt
Dockerfile

Then your Dockerfile can do:

COPY . /app

This means:

Copy the files from the build context into the Docker image.

Simple memory
docker build -t myapp .
                         ↑
                  Build context

. = current directory = build context.
Important

The Dockerfile doesn't have to be in the same directory. You can specify another location:

docker build -f docker/Dockerfile -t myapp:v1 .

Here:

myapp/
├── app.py
├── requirements.txt
└── docker/
    └── Dockerfile


For example:
myapp/
├── Dockerfile
├── Dockerfile.dev
├── Dockerfile.prod
├── app.py
└── requirements.txt

Here you have 3 Dockerfiles.
How to build each one

Default Dockerfile:   docker build -t myapp .
Dockerfile.dev:       docker build -f Dockerfile.dev -t myapp:dev .
Dockerfile.prod:  docker build -f Dockerfile.prod -t myapp:prod .

Here -f means:  Tell Docker which Dockerfile to use.
